 # Incident log
 
 Newest first. Started 2026-08-10; nothing before that date was written up.
+
+## 2026-09-22 — Two-thirds of our reopens were requirements already on the ticket, in a comment
+
+**What happened.** A read of the 12 closed tickets created March to July 2026
+(`tickets.jsonl`). 9 were reopened. In 6 of those 9, product or design posted
+the deciding requirement as a comment on or before the day the ticket went In
+Progress: recent-first sort for large accounts (INV-101), text on status
+badges (INV-104), a first-invoice card on empty dashboards (INV-107), locale
+dates (INV-110), Bank details hidden from Viewers (INV-113), and a collapsed
+filter bar on phones (INV-116). The exported description's AC doesn't contain
+any of them. Every pre-start product/design comment in the window held a
+requirement, and 6 of 6 were missed. On INV-101 and INV-104 the developer
+said they built "per AC". QA caught 4 of the 6. On INV-104 and INV-110 QA
+signed off with "Matches AC" / "Passes AC", and customers found the problems.
+On INV-104, six weeks passed between QA sign-off and a screen-reader user's
+report.
+
+**Why nothing caught it.** The comments did not arrive late, since every one
+predates the start of work. Nobody was careless either. The process defines
+the requirement as the AC, and the definition of done said "Acceptance
+criteria in the description pass", so both the build and QA's check were
+aimed at a document that lacked the requirement. After work starts, the
+comment stream is mostly status and priority notes (INV-102, INV-108,
+INV-117), which teaches people that comments are not where scope lives. The
+developers' closing comments describe the symptom ("Sorted alphabetically
+instead of recent-first") and never mention the comment, so no single ticket
+shows the pattern. 5 of 12 tickets have no label, and the rest carry only a
+category, so the tracker could not surface this theme.
+
+**Limits of the evidence.** The export has the final description only, with
+no edit history, so "the AC never had it" is measured at close. It is
+consistent with the fixes, but it is not proven for the moment work started.
+Only closed tickets were exported. Requirements given verbally, in chat, or
+only in a design frame leave no comment and are not counted. INV-104 and
+INV-116 also had the requirement in a design frame.
+
+**Rule.** A requirement is in the AC or it does not exist. Whoever raises a
+requirement in a comment copies it into the AC. Before moving to In
+Progress, the developer confirms that every product/design comment is either
+in the AC or marked "no change to AC". This is template and DoD text, not a
+check, and nothing enforces it yet. A mechanical version would block the move
+to In Progress while a product/design comment has no AC reconciliation. It
+has not been built.
+
+Rest of the window, not acted on yet:
+- Two tickets where one word meant two things: "hidden" in INV-102 and
+  "active" in INV-114, both reaching users. INV-110's "like everywhere else"
+  points the same way: product-wide conventions that exist only in
+  product's head.
+- Two states nobody specified: offline save in INV-105 and keyboard access in
+  INV-117.
+- One test that asked a cheaper question: INV-108 checked for a 200 status,
+  not the rows.
+- One rule written only after a second path diverged: INV-111.
+
+```
